  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Plutarch\u2019s Cosmological Ethics is showing early scholarly uptake and meaningful usage. The book has been cited once, has 1,492 recorded downloads, and is openly available in hybrid form, which supports reach across both subscription and open-access channels.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Bram Demulder\u2019s book makes a focused intervention in ancient philosophy by reappraising Plutarch\u2019s ethics through the lens of Plato\u2019s cosmology. The record suggests that this argument is already finding readers: the book has been downloaded 1,492 times through OAPEN-tracked access, indicating sustained consultation rather than only one-off attention.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still early but visible. OpenAlex records one citation, in a 2024 article in Peitho Examina Antiqua. That citation profile is modest, but it does show that the book has entered the research conversation and is being used in later work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s reach is also strengthened by its accessibility. OpenAIRE identifies it as hybrid open access, with multiple open instances and availability in Belgium and the Netherlands. This wider availability likely supports both research use and library circulation, and the WorldCat record indicates discoverability in library systems.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Thematically, the book sits at the intersection of philosophy, cosmology, epistemology, and ethics, with an SDG alignment to Peace, Justice and strong institutions. It was also supported by KU Leuven. Overall, the evidence points to a work that is already being read and cited, with its strongest impact signal currently coming from usage and its emerging scholarly influence beginning to register in the literature.</p>",
